WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.280
What do Batman and William Shakespeare have in&nbsp;
common? It's not a riddle – just an Easter egg,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:09.840
one of many in Matt Reeves' "The Batman."&nbsp;
Warning: spoilers for "The Batman" ahead.

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:10.800
A ghoulish gang

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:14.960
The earliest action set-piece in "The&nbsp;
Batman" comes fairly early on. Beforehand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:19.040
we see a gang dressed in ghoulish black-and-white&nbsp;
face makeup target a man, but before they can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:23.120
cause any real harm, Batman emerges from&nbsp;
the shadows, ready to lay down the hurt.

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:29.840
"The hell are you supposed to be?"

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:34.320
The gang members never provide a name for&nbsp;
themselves, and it's possible they only wore&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:39.040
the face makeup because it was Halloween, but it's&nbsp;
hard not to see a few influences on the aesthetic.

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:42.800
First and foremost, there's the mutant gang&nbsp;
that Batman fights in Frank Miller's "The Dark&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:48.080
Knight Returns." They also boasted pasty-white&nbsp;
skin, reminiscent of the gang in the new movie.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:51.440
Plus, there's the Jokerz gang from&nbsp;
"Batman Beyond," who dressed up as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:54.720
their favorite supervillain. Thanks to a&nbsp;
brief cameo toward the end of the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:59.120
we know Joker exists in this universe, so could&nbsp;
he already have some minions at his disposal?

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:02.880
It's also worth mentioning the DC-related&nbsp;
appearance in the gang fight scene.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:05.840
The young kid who's trying to become a&nbsp;
member of the gang who refuses to commit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:09.440
violence is played by Jay Lycurgo,&nbsp;
who plays Tim Drake on "Titans."

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:10.480
"I'm vengeance"

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:14.880
While on the subject of the opening fight, those&nbsp;
thugs apparently didn't know who they were messing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:19.280
with. Right as Batman arrives on the scene, he&nbsp;
lays down some hurt upon being asked who he is.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:22.560
After laying down the hurt, Batman&nbsp;
responds in a way only Batman can:

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:24.560
"I'm vengeance."

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:29.120
It's a cool line, and it also comes straight&nbsp;
from "Batman: The Animated Series" in the 1990s.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:32.320
While one might assume the line would&nbsp;
come from the pages of a comic book,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:35.760
the first time he actually uttered the phrase&nbsp;
came in the episode, "Nothing to Fear,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:40.000
where Batman first runs into Scarecrow. At&nbsp;
the end of the storyline, Batman's able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:44.000
overcome his fears, and in the process, he&nbsp;
lays down the pep talk to end all pep talks:

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:51.600
"I am vengeance! I am the night! I am Batman!"

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:54.880
Robert Pattinson's Batman&nbsp;
may stop at the first third,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:56.960
but it's chilling to hear him say it nonetheless.

00:01:56.960 --> 00:01:57.840
Jerry Robinson

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:00.960
There's a lot happening during the&nbsp;
explosive car chase between Batman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:04.400
and Penguin. With so much going on, you'd&nbsp;
be forgiven if you missed a quick shoutout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:07.680
to one of the most influential&nbsp;
parties of the Dark Knight mythos.

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:11.200
At one point during the chase, you can see a&nbsp;
street sign directing traffic to go somewhere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:16.560
called "Robinson Bridge." This is more than likely&nbsp;
a reference to Jerry Robinson. He was a primary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:21.200
ghost artist for Batman comics throughout the&nbsp;
1940s, working closely with both Bob Kane and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:25.120
Bill Finger to develop many of the central&nbsp;
tenets that went on to define the character.

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:28.800
He's most often credited with helping&nbsp;
to create Robin the Boy Wonder.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:31.680
He also claimed to have created&nbsp;
the Joker, reportedly drawing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:35.840
first concept sketch for the character&nbsp;
– but Bob Kane repeatedly disputed that.

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:39.280
Regardless of who created what, Jerry&nbsp;
Robinson was central to defining the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:43.840
character with many of his contributions still&nbsp;
cropping up in Batman adaptations to this day.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:47.760
It's nice to see a shout out to the legendary&nbsp;
artist in the latest iteration of the character.

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:48.400
Nods to '66

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:54.000
Director Matt Reeves is apparently quite a big&nbsp;
fan of the original 1966 "Batman" TV series.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:59.520
Prior to shooting "The Batman," in 2018, he&nbsp;
attended a "Batman" '66 retrospective exhibit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:02.240
uploading a plethora of photos&nbsp;
from his visit to Twitter.

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:06.560
That influence seems to have carried&nbsp;
over into "The Batman." For starters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:10.640
you can find a bust of William Shakespeare in the&nbsp;
background of Bruce Wayne's office. This is an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:15.840
allusion to 1966's "Batman" where Bruce Wayne,&nbsp;
played by Adam West, would access the Batcave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:20.720
through a secret switch that he kept hidden within&nbsp;
a bust of, you guessed it, William Shakespeare.

00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:25.360
But that's not all. Later in the movie, Batman&nbsp;
runs down the side of a building using a cable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:30.560
This is another allusion to 1966's "Batman" as&nbsp;
the Caped Crusader and the Boy Wonder Robin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:34.240
played by Burt Ward, would often scale&nbsp;
the side of buildings using a cable.

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:38.160
"Hey, who are you?" "He&nbsp;
must be new in Gotham City."

